// An INFERRED-empty list that a `.push` fills with STRUCT literals, with NO `<shape>[]`
// SPELLING ANYWHERE in the module.
//
// CLOSED by D1511 (`arrLitShapeElemName` + `collectA`'s empty-literal shape arm): the
// ref-list ROW for the element now exists, so the annotation `synthEmptyListAnn` was already
// synthesizing resolves to a real slot instead of falling back to the i32-list default.
//
// WHAT USED TO DECIDE IT WAS ANOTHER LINE ENTIRELY. A single `P[]` spelling anywhere — a
// parameter, a return, a second annotated binding — minted the row and the whole family ran.
// So the reproduction was: delete the unrelated annotation.
//
//   emitProgram: field access receiver is not a struct       (the read)
//   type mismatch: expected i32, found (ref $type)           (`.push` alone, check-clean)
//
// The second is why the row is clause 1 as well as clause 2: two lines with no read at all
// were a silently invalid module.
//
// ANNOTATING THE BINDING FIXED IT, which is exactly why the sibling fixture
// `tests/cases/arrays/infer-build-struct.vl` — which annotates every destination `P[]` —
// could not see this defect (D969's rule).
//
// Reported as VL-017 by the first external consumer (`~/glean/docs/vl-issues.md`).
//
// Should print: 1 1 1 1 2
const xs = []
xs.push({ y: 1, x: 2 })
print(xs[0].y)

const ys = []
ys.push({ y: 1, x: 2 })
const p = ys[0]
print(p.y)

const zs = []
zs.push({ y: 1, x: 2 })
for q in zs { print(q.y) }

function inFn() {
  const ws = []
  ws.push({ y: 1, x: 2 })
  print(ws[0].y)
}
inFn()

// The list itself, not just a read of it: two pushes and the length prove the wrapper is the
// ref-list one and not the i32 backing a mis-repped binding would have taken.
const ms = []
ms.push({ y: 1, x: 2 })
ms.push({ y: 3, x: 4 })
print(ms.length)
